# Content Management - Marketer MCP First, REST Only for Gaps

## The rule

Any agent that MANAGES Sitecore content - creating/editing pages, datasources, field
values, placing/removing components, media, and page composition - uses the
**Marketer MCP** (`mcp__sitecore-marketer__*`) as the first and default mechanism. Raw
**Authoring GraphQL / SitecoreAI REST** is a **fallback only** for operations the Marketer
MCP does not expose. This applies to BOTH content surfaces:

- **Developer (08)** during `/implement` Phase 6 live content-model authoring.
- **Content Author (14)** during `/author-content` and `/test-content` fixes.

## BEFORE the first MCP write — probe the BINDING (where), not just the result (what)

Everything else in this rule verifies **what** was written. None of it verifies **where**. The
Marketer MCP endpoint (`https://marketer.sitecorecloud.io/mcp/marketer-mcp-prod`) carries **no
tenant, no environment and no host** — the write target comes entirely from the authenticated
session, so it is unassertable from anything readable. `assert-tenant.mjs` does **not** cover
this channel and must not be extended to: its guarantee is *derive the host from the claim of a
token I just minted*, and the agent mints no token here. It also discriminates at the **tenant**
level, one grain coarser than the failure below.

**So probe the binding before the first content write of a session:**

1. `create_content_item` **one throwaway item**.
2. Read it back through **two independent instruments** — the verified Authoring GraphQL (host
   derived via `assert-tenant.mjs`) and Experience Edge — each with a **known-good and a
   known-bad control**.
3. `delete_content` it and **confirm absence by re-read** on both.

**A read-back through the tool that wrote it proves nothing.** Neither does an identity check on
existing content: pre-existing items cross-resolve by GUID across environments of one estate
(items-as-resources — SCS preserves GUIDs), so path, id and read-back all agree on the wrong
target. **Only a NEW DB item discriminates.**

**The controls are the load-bearing half, and the rule does not depend on the transport.** A
sweep where *everything* comes back absent — the known-good control included — is a claim about
your harness, not an answer about the estate: discard it, repair the instrument, re-run. Note
the Edge context id's transport is recorded **inconsistently** in this tree and is deliberately
left open: `sitecoreai-edge-graphql-capture`'s `capture-edge.mjs` builds
`?sitecoreContextId=<ctx>` and works, while the 2026-09-27 run below recorded an
`x-sitecore-contextid` **header**, with a query-param attempt returning uniform nulls. The
param spellings differ, so both can be true of their own endpoint — and a uniform-null result is
the documented signature of a broken harness either way. Do not settle it by assertion; the
probe above is correct under both.

**Measured 2026-09-27 (a prior run's FRD).** `create_content_item` returned a
correct path and id and read back perfectly **through the MCP**, while the verified Authoring
GraphQL and Experience Edge both showed the item **absent** — the session was bound to a
different environment of the same estate. Nine content items were written there before anyone
noticed, and they were left in place, because deleting is teardown on an estate you cannot
identify.

**Record the answer** in the run manifest at `provisioning.mcp_binding` — the resolved
environment plus the instrument evidence. Not a gate, a record: "which estate did this run's
content go to?" had no answer afterwards, which is the whole reason those nine items are still
sitting somewhere.

> **Status — this is a manual precondition, not a gate.** Nothing asserts it today. The
> mechanical half (a two-instrument, four-control probe script on the `probe-icon.mjs` shape,
> exit 2 on a degraded harness rather than a verdict) is designed
> and is **not built**. Saying otherwise would be an overstated enforcement claim, which is the
> specific thing that makes the next agent skip a check it believes is already covered.

## Why (the load-bearing failure)

Pages **created via raw Authoring GraphQL `createItem`** (hand-writing the `__Renderings`
XML) come out **structurally identical to a working page yet DO NOT ROUTE**: the CM layout
service resolves no route, `getPage` returns a page whose `layout.sitecore.route` is
`undefined`, and the head app 500s (build goes red). Proven on a prior run, 2026-07-06 - even a
byte-exact duplicate of the working Home page, created via raw `createItem`, returned
`route=NONE`, while the sibling site's template-created pages resolved fine. Deleting those
pages and recreating them with the Marketer MCP `create_page` made them route immediately
(HTTP 200). The MCP performs the page-creation ceremony (route/layout wiring, projection)
that hand-rolled `createItem` skips. This is a recurring class of tenant pain; the fix is
procedural - create pages the MCP way. (Same root class as raw `__Renderings` writes not
projecting to Edge.)

## The correct pattern

- **Create pages** with `create_page` (templateId + parentId + name). Never `createItem` for a page: a raw-created page does not route.
- **Place components** with `add_component_on_page`; **attach datasources** with
  `set_component_datasource`; **set field values** with `update_fields_on_item` /
  `update_content`; **add child items** with `create_content_item`; **delete** with `delete_content`.
- **Datasource placement — an EXPLICIT decision per datasource (local vs global), never an
  assumed default.** Both homes are always available: Sitecore always offers the page's own
  **`./Data`** folder, and the rendering's `Datasource Location` field adds the **global**
  per-component container under the site `Data` node as an ADDITIONAL root (rule `80` § 0
  item 3) — it does not remove the local option or decide which one wins. Decide **before**
  the write:
  - **page-specific → LOCAL**, under `<Page>/Data` (folder template `Page Data`
    `{1C82E550-EBCD-4E5D-8ABD-D50D0809541E}`, created on demand). **This is the default** —
    when unsure, stay local.
  - **genuinely reused on ≥2 pages → GLOBAL**, in the component's folder under the site
    `Data` node (built from the component's folder template), authored **once** and bound to
    each placement with `set_component_datasource`. Give it a self-descriptive name — the
    name is all an author sees in a shared picker.
  - **partial-design chrome (Header / Footer / promo + utility bars) → always local** to the
    partial design, never global. It is chrome, not reused content.

  The MCP has **no move/rename primitive**, so a wrong placement means re-creating the item:
  make the call in the page **plan** — a cross-page reuse pass over the planned datasources
  *before* authoring — not per component mid-write.

  **The two recipes (probe-verified on a live tenant, 2026-07-27):**

  - **LOCAL = `add_component_on_page(…, fields)` and nothing else.** It *always* writes
    `<Page>/Data/<componentItemName>` (`local:/Data/X` in the layout) and auto-creates the
    page's `Data` folder — it **ignores** the rendering's global `Datasource Location`. Spaces
    in `componentItemName` become underscores. One call, no cleanup.
  - **GLOBAL = create the item in the container, then rebind, then delete the stray.**
    `create_content_item(name, templateId: <DS template>, parentId: <the component's global
    container item>)` → `add_component_on_page(…)` → `set_component_datasource(pageId,
    componentId, datasourceId)` → `delete_content(<the local datasource the add just made>)`.
    **There is no way to add a component without producing a local datasource** — omitting
    `fields` still creates an empty one — so the stray delete is a mandatory step, not an
    optional tidy-up.
  - **Reuse on page 2..n:** `search_component_ds(component_id, term)` → **filter the hits by
    path** (it searches by datasource template and returns *local* items too, not just shared
    ones) → then the same add → rebind → delete-stray sequence.

  **Two traps:** `set_component_datasource`'s `componentId` is the rendering **instance** id
  (`components[].id` from `get_components_on_page`), **not** the rendering id
  (`components[].componentId`) — both are in the same payload. And `create_component_ds` does
  place globally + returns `datasourceLocation`, but takes **no name** — it auto-names
  `<Component>_<32-hex>`, which is unusable for an item whose whole point is being picked by
  name from a shared list. Prefer `create_content_item` with an explicit `parentId`.

  **Invariant: a local datasource's parent is `<Page>/Data`, never the page item itself.**
  This holds for every mechanism, not just the MCP: `add_component_on_page`, `create_content_item`,
  raw `createItem`, and a `moveItem` that demotes a global datasource to local. `<Page>/Data` is a
  real item (template `Page Data` `{1C82E550-EBCD-4E5D-8ABD-D50D0809541E}`) — **create it if
  absent**, then parent to it.

  **The trap: only `add_component_on_page` auto-creates the `Data` folder.**
  `create_content_item` does **not** — it parents exactly where you point it, so
  `create_content_item(parentId: <the PAGE>)` lands the datasource **bare under the page** and
  nothing complains. That path is not hypothetical: it is what you fall into whenever
  `set_component_datasource` is unavailable (it 422s on renderings created via raw AGQL), because the
  flow degrades to `create_content_item` + a raw layout-level attach. **One site shipped 35
  datasources this way** (2026-08-05) — every page rendered, build and tests were green, and a
  rendering-level schema audit scored 15/15 clean, because `Datasource Location` read the literal
  `./Data` pointing at a folder that never existed. Only an operator reading the content tree saw it.

  **When MOVING a datasource to local page level** (global → local, or re-homing between pages):
  the target parent is `<Page>/Data`, created on demand — never the page. A Sitecore `moveItem`
  preserves the item GUID and layout references are by GUID, so relocating is safe, but it is a
  live content mutation: canary one page and re-assert the route before rolling.

  **Verify either way — assert the PARENT, not just existence.** Read the created/moved item's
  path back and confirm its parent segment is `Data`. "The item exists" and "the field is
  non-empty" both pass on the broken placement. See `sitecoreai-content-composition`
  § 'Local vs global datasources'.
- **Media** via the MCP 2-step `uploadMedia`; link with `update_fields_on_item`.
- **Publish** is separate + explicit (the MCP has NO publish tool) -> Publishing REST
  (`sitecoreai-publishing`).
- **Re-auth over fallback.** If the Marketer MCP is disconnected / its token expired
  (403 - happens every few minutes), **ASK THE OPERATOR TO RE-AUTHORIZE IT** (`/mcp` ->
  reconnect `sitecore-marketer`) and continue on the MCP. A token-in-the-loop workflow
  (operator re-auths as needed) is the intended flow. Do NOT silently drop to raw GraphQL
  to dodge a re-auth - falling back is only for a genuine tool GAP, never an auth hiccup.

## Multi-value fields are pipe-delimited, and the write is read back

A multi-value field value (Multilist, Treelist, Checklist, `PartialDesigns`, …) is a
single string of GUIDs joined by **`|`**:

```
{DD26D1E7-C51D-4B36-957C-81AB2D678168}|{A3AD9C45-7669-469E-9108-6B8F4A78DFCE}
```

**A newline-joined value is accepted, stored, and read back verbatim — and resolves to
EMPTY in delivery.** Every check an agent naturally reaches for passes:

| check | result on a newline-separated value |
|---|---|
| the write call | succeeds, HTTP 200 |
| read the field back | returns exactly what you wrote |
| `audit-sitecore-schema.mjs` | clean — it reads the **disk**, and SCS serializes a *correct*
  pipe-delimited value as one GUID per line, so the on-disk form is **identical either way** |
| `audit-component-authorability.mjs` | clean — the component renders an SDK field component |
| the component | renders, with **zero** items |

So the defect is invisible to every gate in the framework and survives to production
looking like a styling problem. Measured on a prior run: `SelectedWork.Entries` held all
six entries and delivered none.

**Therefore, on every multi-value write:**

1. **Join with `|`.** Never `\n`, never `, `. Build the string explicitly rather than
   letting a language's default array-to-string conversion pick a separator.
2. **Read the field back from the LIVE tenant and assert BOTH** — `!/\n/.test(value)` **and**
   the GUID count equals what you intended. Counting is not optional: splitting a
   newline-joined value on `|` yields **1**, which reads as "truncated to one entry" and
   sends you debugging the wrong thing.
3. **Assert the rendered ITEM COUNT** in the e2e layer — "SelectedWork renders 6 entries".
   This is the cheapest real gate and the only one that needs no Sitecore knowledge.

Do **not** try to catch this with a disk-based audit; it structurally cannot see it.

## Legitimate REST / Authoring-GraphQL fallback (the narrow exceptions)

Use raw Authoring GraphQL / REST ONLY where the MCP has no equivalent tool, e.g.:

- **Templates / schema** - datasource templates, rendering definitions, resolvers. The
  Agent API has no create-template / create-rendering endpoint, so schema authoring over
  raw AGQL is expected.
- **Component / rendering VARIANT selection** where the MCP lacks a variant tool.
- Low-level presentation the MCP cannot express.

Record every fallback (what + why the MCP could not do it) in the run report so it is auditable.

## Agent application

- **08 Developer:** In Phase 6 live authoring, create pages + place components + set content
  via the Marketer MCP first (08 now carries `mcp__sitecore-marketer__*`). Raw AGQL only for
  datasource templates / renderings / resolvers (schema) and variant wiring. If you are about
  to hand-write a `__Renderings` XML for a NEW page, STOP - use `create_page`.
  - **Schema is built PER COMPONENT during `/implement`, not deferred.** Each component's
    rendering + datasource template(s) + resolver/`ComponentQuery` are created live via raw
    AGQL **in the same tranche as its code** (the sanctioned schema path above), then
    `ser pull`-ed into `cm/` at the tranche's delivery-loop exit — see rule `80` § 0 for the
    per-component Definition-of-Done + persistence. Deferring schema to "assembly" leaves
    `add_component_on_page` with no rendering to place (a prior run's PRD-000, 2026-07-12).
- **14 Content Author:** Same - the Marketer MCP is the way for all page/datasource/child
  content; re-auth rather than fall back.
- **06 Lead Developer / 07 QA:** A content-authoring task's plan/tests assume MCP-authored
  pages. A page that 500s with `route` undefined is the raw-`createItem` smell - flag it.

